Point sfu_boot.c's interrupt header at this family.

sfu_boot.c:35 reads `#include "stm32l4xx_it.h"`.  That is the only place in
ST's SBSFU application sources that names an STM32 family, and so the only
one a port cannot resolve through configuration or the include path --
everything else reaches the target through main.h or through the Target/
directory, both of which this port owns.

IT IS NOT A DEAD INCLUDE HERE, which is where this port and the SAM D21 one
diverge.  That port's 0001 patch DELETES the line, having checked that
sfu_boot.c uses nothing from the header.  On this build it is used: ST
redirects the memory-fault vector into sfu_boot.c with

    sfu_low_level_security.h:299
      #define SFU_CALLBACK_MEMORYFAULT(void) MemManage_Handler(void)

so sfu_boot.c:1592 DEFINES MemManage_Handler, and the header is what
declares it first.  Substituting the family keeps that prototype;
Core/Inc/stm32l1xx_it.h supplies it, and Core/Src/stm32l1xx_it.c defines
the handlers ST's boot module does not claim.

Applied by the Makefile's STAGE_RULE with -F0, so if ST's line ever moves
the build stops rather than patching something approximate into a
bootloader.

--- sfu_boot.c
+++ sfu_boot.c
@@ -32,7 +32,7 @@
 #include "sfu_low_level.h"
 #include "sfu_fsm_states.h"
 #include "sfu_error.h"
-#include "stm32l4xx_it.h" /* required for the HAL Cube callbacks */
+#include "stm32l1xx_it.h" /* required for the HAL Cube callbacks */
 /*
  * The sfu_com init is provided by the sfu_com_trace module by default.
  * If not, then it is taken from the sfu_com_loader module.
